            <div xml:id="d419">

                <pb facs="0839" n="795" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v05p1/pg795"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>European unity and early ratification of the EDC Treaty were discussed at the
                    Meetings of the Foreign Ministers of the United States, United Kingdom, and
                    France at Washington, July 10 to 14, 1953. Throughout the discussions, Lord
                    Salisbury of the United Kingdom and Secretary Dulles continually reiterated
                    their country’s keen desire to see an early ratification of the EDC, and both
                    men reminded Bidault of the strenuous exertions made on behalf of European unity
                    by the United States and United Kingdom since the close of World War II. <pb
                        facs="0840" n="796" xml:id="pg796"/> Bidault, in turn, continually expressed
                    recognition of the impatience generated in the United States and United Kingdom
                    by French hesitancy to ratify, reiterated the many problems which stood in the
                    way of early ratification by the French National Assembly, and pledged to work
                    for ratification as soon as feasible.</p>
                <p> Lord Salisbury and Secretary Dulles, in turn, pledged that proposed Four-Power
                    talks with the Soviet Union concerning the future of Germany would not be
                    permitted to re-open the question of EDC. For documentation on the meetings of
                    the Foreign Ministers, see <ref target="frus:frus1952-54v05p2/pg_1582">pages
                        1582</ref> ff.</p>
            </div>
